## 3. Supply Chain and Dependency Exploits## Malicious Transitive Dependency Injection (Software Supply Chain)

* How it works: Modern websites are built on top of thousands of open-source libraries. If an attacker identifies a popular low-level package used by financial websites, they can compromise the developer's repository or publish a malicious update version (a supply chain attack).
* Autonomous Execution: The attacker inserts a stealthy backdoor inside a routine code update. When the financial website's automated build systems run their nightly updates (e.g., via npm update or pip install), they pull in the compromised library version.
* The Cash-out: The malicious library runs with full server-side permissions. It sits silently inside the website, automatically altering the destination bank routing numbers of every single withdrawal processed by the site, rerouting thousands of dollars into attacker-controlled mule accounts without changing the transaction history visible to users. [21, 22] 

